     which means that if a regular expression matches another defined mount point,
     it will override the previously defined threshold.
 
+    A mount can also be given a dict of ``min``/``max`` thresholds instead of a
+    single percentage, to additionally (or instead) alert when usage drops
+    below what is expected. This is normally a sign that the filesystem failed
+    to mount, and what looks like the mount point is actually just an empty
+    directory on the root filesystem, rather than that space was genuinely
+    freed up.
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                min: 20%
+                max: 90%
+
+    ``min`` and ``max`` are each optional, so a mount can be configured with
+    only a low-usage alert:
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                min: 20%
+
